#!/bin/sh
# A DEPLOY IS NOT A PLACE TO COMMIT.
#
#   git config core.hooksPath tools/hooks      # once per clone; hooks are shared by every worktree
#
# WHAT THIS REFUSES: a commit made in a worktree whose HEAD is detached at a `deploy-*` tag.
# That is what a production checkout looks like, and committing there is how a deploy ends up
# AHEAD of the trunk — work that exists only in the deploy, reachable from nothing, and silently
# discarded the next time the worktree is moved onto a tag.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-11. Prod went ahead of the trunk THREE times in one night:
#   * twice by an agent outside the session graph committing straight into prod-deploy
#     (`codex/tactical-backend-repair`, `codex/audio-intent-capture`), each time leaving two
#     commits reachable only from a detached HEAD;
#   * once by a session cutting `deploy-2026-09-11-3` from a local trunk line that had never
#     been pushed.
# All three were caught by `m59-deploy.mjs --verify` AFTER the fact, and each cost a manual
# adoption to get the work back onto main.
#
# WHY A HOOK RATHER THAN A RULE. CLAUDE.md has said "never check a branch out in the prod
# worktree" for months and it is the right rule. It is also unenforceable against a committer
# that cannot read it: the agent that did this twice is not a Claude session, does not appear in
# ListAgents, and cannot be messaged. A hook is the only instruction such a party receives.
#
# WHY THE TAG AND NOT THE PATH. Keying on "is this prod-deploy" would protect one directory and
# miss the next deploy worktree somebody makes. Standing on a `deploy-*` tag IS the definition of
# a deploy artifact, so the guard travels with the state rather than the location.
#
# THE ESCAPE HATCH IS DELIBERATE AND LOUD. `git commit --no-verify` still works, because a hook
# that cannot be bypassed gets uninstalled by the first person it blocks at 02:00. What this
# removes is committing there BY ACCIDENT, which is every one of the three incidents above.

head_ref=$(git symbolic-ref -q HEAD 2>/dev/null)
if [ -n "$head_ref" ]; then
  exit 0        # on a branch: an ordinary working checkout, nothing to say
fi

tag=$(git describe --tags --exact-match HEAD 2>/dev/null)
case "$tag" in
  deploy-*)
    root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)
    cat >&2 <<EOF

REFUSING A COMMIT IN A DEPLOY CHECKOUT.

  worktree   $root
  HEAD       detached at $tag

A deploy is a versioned, read-only checkout of the trunk. A commit here is reachable from
nothing but this worktree's HEAD: it is invisible to main, it makes the deploy AHEAD of the
trunk, and it is discarded the next time this worktree is moved onto a tag.

What to do instead:

  1. commit the change on main, in your own worktree
         git worktree add ../work-<name> -b <branch> origin/main
  2. push it
  3. cut a new tag and move this worktree onto it
         node tools/m59-deploy.mjs --cut

If you have already made changes here, they are still in the working tree. Copy them out
before anyone moves this checkout — nothing else will preserve them.

  git -C "$root" diff > /tmp/deploy-wip.patch

If you genuinely mean it, \`git commit --no-verify\` is not blocked. Prod going ahead of the
trunk happened three times on 2026-09-11 and cost a hand adoption each time, so please do not
mean it by accident.

EOF
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

exit 0
